{quote}If we dropped it, feel free to mark as deprioritised/ won’t solve{quote}

I’ll bring it up with Rajat, but it felt agreed between him and I. Since it’s not a product metric, I’d be comfortable letting it go, though a quick sync with Product might help confirm.

My main hesitation is that we can’t reliably identify this data at the driver level. Keeping the metric could imply we have access to something we don’t, beyond a few manual inputs. I find it hard to draw meaningful insights from this.



If we do not drop it: 

{quote}If Ops is already tracking it it should be easier and we just need to replicate it in dbt I guess.{quote}

They have data from a few external partners via spreadsheets. Replicating that in dbt would need a manual, ongoing process—prone to errors and hard to maintain. And also outside of the DBT metrics week scope imo. 

If we decide to do this solution, we can try aiming to get it done by EOM August?

{quote}For example, cash back can directly be converted to GMV{quote}

That’s only part of the picture we can somewhat convert to GMV. Internal benefits drive most of the claim rate, and cashback is maybe half of those. So overall, we’d be working with a metric that’s only (really back of the napkin calculation) ~40% accurate.

Given the limited visibility and accuracy, I’m not sure it’s worth keeping the metric.

