Hi [~accountid:62615f256cb53d006a6a3fe1].

I have written a follow up email to Deloitte with your query and I received the following response:

{color:#0747a6}We understand the difference between the example outlined below and the initial scenario would be that:{color}

* {color:#0747a6}in the initial scenario, the driver would charge the same amount for the trip (£10) and Bolt would essentially ‘part pay’ for the journey, meaning Bolt would only actually collect £8 from the passenger and would make this up to the driver by reducing the commission it would otherwise have charged in respect of the journey (in this example, if the commission would otherwise have been £2 then Bolt simply wouldn’t charge the commission at all).  In the context of Project Blue in 2023 we discussed this being presented as coupons/vouchers being offered by Bolt to passengers for part payment (we would refer you to the ‘Counsel Table of Factors’ which was produced by Valentina Sloane KC and Philip Simpson KC); however,{color}
* {color:#0747a6}what you’re proposing below is that the driver agrees to reduce the price of their services to the passenger (so only invoicing the customer £8) and Bolt compensates the driver for agreeing to make that price reduction by reducing its commission.{color}

{color:#0747a6}As a preliminary point, this would present a {color}{color:#0747a6}+significant risk to the agency analysis as a whole+{color}{color:#0747a6} as it seems to be a clear example of Bolt controlling the price of the services being supplied by the driver to the passenger.{color}

{color:#0747a6}If the driver is agreeing with Bolt to reduce its charge to the passenger in return for some form of financial compensation from Bolt, in whatever form that financial compensation takes, including reducing the commission Bolt would otherwise charge, then this would most likely be viewed by HMRC as a VAT-able supply by the driver to Bolt for the value of that reduction in commission (so if the commission is normally £2 and Bolt offers a reduction of £2, the driver would be making a supply to Bolt of £2 offsetting Bolt’s supply of £2).  If the driver is VAT registered then the reduced commission would be inclusive of VAT charged by the driver (and they would need to issue a VAT invoice in respect of this) and that VAT would be input tax which would effectively reduce the net tax payable by Bolt on the overall transaction (so Bolt would charge 33p of VAT on its supply as output tax which would be offset by the 33p of input tax charged by the driver); however, if the driver isn’t VAT registered then there would be no input tax to offset against the output tax due on Bolt’s normal commission.{color}

Best,

Adrian

