{color:#403294}*REVIEW OF MOU AGREEMENT: LAMBETH DOCKLESS BIKE SCHEME*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Contracting Parties:*{color}
1. The London Borough of Lambeth ("the Local Authority"); and
2. Bolt (as the Operator) - entity details need to be filled out. 

{color:#0747a6}*Term: Clause: Part C, Section 9*{color}

* The agreement begins on the Start Date and lasts for *12 months* ("Scheme Period").
* May be extended via new agreement. If not renewed in time, terms continue temporarily until replaced or terminated.
* Either party may terminate participation with *90 days' written notice* before the end of the Scheme Period.

{color:#0747a6}*Fees Paid By Bolt*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: Part A (Key Terms)*{color}

* {color:#0747a6}*Annual Access Fee (based on fleet tier):*{color}
** *Tier 1 (up to 500 bikes): £85,000*
** Tier 2 (1,000 bikes): £170,000
** Tier 3 (1,500 bikes): £255,000
* Bolt will start at *Tier 1* given Bolt is a new operator.
* Council will invoice based on the number of bays and fleet tier.
* Payment due within{color:#ff5630} {color}{color:#ff5630}+*30 days*+{color}{color:#ff5630} {color}of invoice. VAT applicable.

{color:#0747a6}*Bolt’s Obligations Under The Agreement*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clauses: Part B, Sections 3 - 4.9*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*General:*{color}

* Operate only within designated "Scheme Area" and "Dockless Bays".
* Deploy a fleet up to the limit of its tier; over 10% excess for >24 hrs without permission is prohibited.

{color:#0747a6}*Service Delivery:*{color}

* Comply with all operational, safety, maintenance, and redistribution duties.
* Bikes must meet technical specifications (Part D), including tracking, lights, brake systems, and 24/7 contact signage.
* Maintain records and inspect for wear/stress.
* Provide real-time app and user education.
* Staff must be adequately resourced locally with a warehouse/operations centre.

{color:#0747a6}*Data & Reporting:*{color}

* Provide near real-time data through a secure dashboard or API.
* Monthly trip/safety/fleet usage reports; quarterly environmental and outreach reports.
* Council may share aggregate insights (with some restrictions).

{color:#0747a6}*Redistribution & Fleet Expansion:*{color}

* Bolt may request a move to a higher tier at annual review. Criteria include:
** Fleet utilisation
** Bike availability
** Parking compliance
** Safety incident response
** Council feedback
* Requests must be made {color:#bf2600}+*at least two months before*+{color} renewal date.

{color:#0747a6}*Priority Areas:*{color}

* Council may identify zones requiring guaranteed availability from *06:30–19:00.*
* Bolt must replenish bikes +*within 48 hours*+ of being notified of shortage.

{color:#0747a6}*No Riding Zones:*{color}

* Bikes must automatically deactivate in these areas.
* 48-hour notice required for changes to any riding/parking/priority zones.

{color:#0747a6}*Public Events:*{color}

* Must cooperate with temporary redistribution and logistics plans. Council may request cost-sharing.

{color:#0747a6}*Environmental Responsibilities:*{color}

* Minimise waste, use ULEZ-compliant/zero-emission vehicles. Avoid pollution or harm to sensitive areas (e.g. waterways).

{color:#0747a6}*Customer Service & Safety:*{color}

* Maintain a 24/7 call centre.
* Train users on proper parking, road safety, and legal usage.
* Enforce penalties for risky behaviours and maintain a rider ban log.

{color:#0747a6}*Servicing Requirements:*{color}

* Faulty or unsafe bikes must be:
** Removed within *24 hours* (e.g. low battery, vandalism)
** Removed within *2 hours* if causing obstruction/hazard

{color:#0747a6}*Council Obligations*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clauses: Part B, 3.1; Part C, 18*{color}

* Provide infrastructure (minimum one bay per 300m).
* Notify Bolt *48 hours in advance* of changes to bays/zones.
* Ringfence Bolt’s fees for dockless infrastructure, road safety, or environment improvements.

{color:#0747a6}*Insurance*{color}

*Clause: Section 15:* Bolt must maintain *public liability insurance of at least £2,000,000* throughout the agreement term.

{color:#0747a6}*Parking Fines & Removals*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: 4.3.2 and Table*{color}

|*Violation*|*Fine*|
|>6 hrs, no obstruction|£30|
|>2 hrs, obstruction|£60|
|>72 hrs|£100/day (confiscation)|
|Immediate hazard|£90 + £10/day after 7 days|

* Removals by Council or Police at Bolt’s cost.
* Bolt has {color:#bf2600}*7 working days*{color} to challenge fines.
* Fines billed monthly; payment due in {color:#bf2600}*14 days*{color}{color:#bf2600}.{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Termination Rights*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: 17*{color}

* Lambeth may terminate:
** If Bolt is in material breach and fails to fix within {color:#bf2600}*30 days*{color}.
** Immediately if Bolt enters insolvency.
** If MoU becomes impractical due to legislation/market change.

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: 4.2 – Additional Risk:*{color}

* Council can revoke deployment rights and forcibly remove bikes if Bolt fails to remedy issues *within 72 hours* of notice.

{color:#0747a6}*Liabilities* {color}

* cost recovery is enforceable: Bolt is liable for storage, confiscation, removal, and damages under enforcement powers (Section 4.3.2.4).

{color:#0747a6}*Assignment & Subcontracting*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: General (Section 22)*{color}

* No express provision for Bolt to assign or subcontract.
* Implicitly restricts transfer; I recommend adding a group entity assignment clause.

{color:#0747a6}*Data Sharing & Privacy*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: Section 12*{color}

* Bolt must comply with *UK GDPR & Data Protection Act 2018*.
* Data shared must be anonymised.
* Council may only use data for non-commercial internal evaluation.

{color:#0747a6}*Publicity*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: Section 21*{color}

* Promotional use allowed if Bolt informs Council and considers feedback.
* Use of Council branding requires consultation.

{color:#0747a6}*Governing Law*{color}

{color:#0747a6}*Clause: Section 22*{color}

* MoU governed by *laws of England and Wales*. Disputes fall under English courts’ jurisdiction.

{color:#0747a6}*Notices*{color}

*Clause: Section 21*

* Notices must be sent in writing by post or email.

{color:#0747a6}*SUGGESTED AMENDMENTS* {color}

*  Insert entity name of Bolt (Bolt Operations UK limited) in full as well as the effective date. 
* Ask Lambeth whether they would be willing to include an assignment clause permitting intra-group transfers. The MoU is currently silent on this point, and it would be helpful to have flexibility in case Bolt changes its operating entity in the future. *See proposed amendment in* {color:#403294}purple {color}*below:* 

{color:#403294}*“*{color}{color:#403294}The Operator shall not assign, transfer, or novate this Agreement or any of its rights or obligations under it to any third party without the prior written consent of the Council, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld or delayed” {color}

{color:#403294}“Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Operator may, with the Council’s prior written consent (not to be unreasonably withheld or delayed), assign or transfer this Agreement to any of its affiliates or group companies, provided that such entity has the requisite financial standing and operational capacity to perform the Operator’s obligations under this Agreement”.{color}

* Request the Council Extend cure period in Clause 4.2 from 72 hours to 5 working days for complex issues.
* Request objective KPIs for tier advancement instead of general criteria.
* Request a pro-rata refund clause for forced tier downgrade if parking bay reductions occur.
* Insert clause confirming subcontracting with written consent (if Bolt needs to delegate logistics).

{color:#0747a6}*SUMMARY* {color}

*Clause 4.2:* {color:#bf2600}+*If Bolt materially or repeatedly breaches service standards tied to CoMoUK accreditation, and fails to fix the issue within 72 hours of written notice, Lambeth can revoke Bolt’s right to operate*+{color}. Which means Bolt may be suspended from deploying bikes, and its fleet may be forcibly removed from the borough at Bolt’s cost.

[~accountid:712020:1d5390cd-af56-43c8-9d12-f431b20183ba] Please take time to read the MoU in full. It sets out a number of obligations on Bolt, and it’s important these align with the commercial terms you’ve agreed with Lambeth.

* In particular, please familiarise yourself with the requirements around parking, fines, service delivery, and operational management. Bolt is also expected to coordinate with other operators on aspects like bay management and redistribution.
* While Bolt’s liability is capped to the annual access fee paid (e.g. £85,000 at Tier 1), this cap +*does not*+ apply to:
* _Death or personal injury due to negligence_
* _Fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation_
* _Other legally non-excludable losses (e.g. statutory liability)_
* Operational penalties, such as parking fines (£30–£100/day), removal fees (£90/bike), and storage costs, {color:#bf2600}+*are not capped*+{color}. These are charged in full and invoiced monthly, with 14-day payment terms (see Clause 4.3.2).
* *Please also ensure the insurance* [~accountid:62a3287576c0360069f59b72] *and privacy teams* [~accountid:712020:0c8dd65b-46d5-4dc0-b5bd-76209c1e8048]  *review their respective sections of the agreement to confirm there are no further issues.*
* If Bolt delivers the service as agreed and rectifies any issues  and makes payments within the specified timeframe, the risk under this MoU remains {color:#36b37e}*low*{color}. However, failure to comply may result in enforcement action, fines, and, if breaches persist - *termination of the agreement without notice*.
* I’d strongly recommend liaising with all relevant internal teams (ops, payments, maintenance, customer service) to ensure smooth delivery and ongoing compliance.
* I’ve suggested some amendments to the draft MoU. It’s worth checking if Lambeth is open to these, though please note they may rely on a standard framework and could push back.
* The key risk here is non-compliance. As long as Bolt upholds its responsibilities, this should remain manageable.

